```toon
spec:

  id: design_principles

  version: 1.0.0

  status: stable

  priority: highest

  module: Core

  compiler_stage: Initialization

  description: >
    Defines the immutable architectural, engineering, and design
    principles governing every component of the TOON Dataset Compiler.
    These principles serve as the constitutional rules for the
    compiler. Every module, specification, generator, validator,
    optimizer, and serializer MUST comply with these principles.

###########################################################
# DESIGN PHILOSOPHY
###########################################################

design:

  philosophy:

    Engineering > Programming

    Knowledge > Tokens

    Graphs > Flat Documents

    Reasoning > Memorization

    Understanding > Pattern Matching

    Quality > Quantity

    Context > Isolated Examples

    Evolution > Snapshots

    Explainability > Black Boxes

###########################################################
# CORE PRINCIPLES
###########################################################

principles:

###########################################################

  P01:

    name:
      Single Source of Truth

    description:
      Every concept must have exactly one authoritative definition.

    objective:
      Eliminate duplication.

###########################################################

  P02:

    name:
      Graph Native

    description:
      Knowledge exists as interconnected graphs,
      never isolated records.

###########################################################

  P03:

    name:
      Engineering First

    description:
      Model engineering reasoning before programming syntax.

###########################################################

  P04:

    name:
      Ontology Driven

    description:
      Every generated object must reference
      the engineering ontology.

###########################################################

  P05:

    name:
      Explicit Reasoning

    description:
      Every important engineering decision must
      contain explicit reasoning.

###########################################################

  P06:

    name:
      Context Preservation

    description:
      Engineering artifacts are meaningless
      without surrounding context.

###########################################################

  P07:

    name:
      Traceability

    description:
      Every generated artifact must be traceable
      to its origin.

###########################################################

  P08:

    name:
      Explainability

    description:
      Every compiler decision must be explainable.

###########################################################

  P09:

    name:
      Determinism

    description:
      Identical inputs must produce identical outputs.

###########################################################

  P10:

    name:
      Modularity

    description:
      Every subsystem should be independently replaceable.

###########################################################

  P11:

    name:
      Extensibility

    description:
      New domains should be added without modifying
      existing architecture.

###########################################################

  P12:

    name:
      Validation Before Acceptance

    description:
      Nothing enters the dataset without validation.

###########################################################

  P13:

    name:
      Repair Before Rejection

    description:
      Attempt automatic repair before discarding records.

###########################################################

  P14:

    name:
      Quality Before Release

    description:
      Every release must satisfy predefined
      quality thresholds.

###########################################################

  P15:

    name:
      Continuous Evolution

    description:
      Knowledge continuously evolves.

###########################################################

  P16:

    name:
      Human Review

    description:
      Critical architectural changes require
      human approval.

###########################################################
# ARCHITECTURAL LAWS
###########################################################

architecture:

  laws:

    Every module has one responsibility.

    Every file has one owner.

    Every dependency is explicit.

    Every interface is documented.

    Every object has a lifecycle.

    Every lifecycle has validation.

    Every validation produces reports.

    Every report is reproducible.

###########################################################
# ENGINEERING LAWS
###########################################################

engineering:

  laws:

    Every problem has context.

    Every solution has assumptions.

    Every assumption has risk.

    Every architecture has tradeoffs.

    Every optimization has cost.

    Every bug has root cause.

    Every lesson becomes reusable knowledge.

###########################################################
# KNOWLEDGE LAWS
###########################################################

knowledge:

  laws:

    Knowledge must be atomic.

    Knowledge must be reusable.

    Knowledge must be composable.

    Knowledge must be linked.

    Knowledge must be versioned.

    Knowledge must remain explainable.

###########################################################
# DATASET LAWS
###########################################################

dataset:

  laws:

    No duplicated knowledge.

    No orphan knowledge.

    No disconnected graphs.

    No unverifiable facts.

    No hidden assumptions.

    No unexplained decisions.

    No synthetic complexity without purpose.

###########################################################
# WORLD SIMULATION LAWS
###########################################################

world:

  laws:

    Every company has history.

    Every repository evolves.

    Every engineer has expertise.

    Every project has constraints.

    Every incident has consequences.

    Every architecture accumulates technical debt.

###########################################################
# GENERATION LAWS
###########################################################

generation:

  laws:

    Never generate isolated code.

    Always generate engineering context.

    Generate realistic constraints.

    Generate multiple alternatives.

    Preserve decision history.

###########################################################
# VALIDATION LAWS
###########################################################

validation:

  laws:

    Validate structure.

    Validate semantics.

    Validate ontology.

    Validate reasoning.

    Validate consistency.

    Validate references.

###########################################################
# OPTIMIZATION LAWS
###########################################################

optimization:

  laws:

    Remove redundancy.

    Preserve information.

    Improve density.

    Maintain readability.

    Never sacrifice correctness.

###########################################################
# SERIALIZATION LAWS
###########################################################

serialization:

  laws:

    Preserve graph structure.

    Preserve relationships.

    Preserve metadata.

    Preserve reasoning.

    Preserve traceability.

###########################################################
# INVARIANTS
###########################################################

invariants:

  Graph Integrity

  Ontology Consistency

  Deterministic Compilation

  Reproducible Outputs

  Version Compatibility

  Explainable Decisions

  Connected Knowledge

  Explicit Metadata

###########################################################
# ANTI-PATTERNS
###########################################################

anti_patterns:

  Duplicate Knowledge

  Circular Dependencies

  Hidden State

  Magic Constants

  Implicit Relationships

  Unvalidated Facts

  Opaque Decisions

  Dead Knowledge

  Broken References

  Overfitting To Benchmarks

###########################################################
# QUALITY ATTRIBUTES
###########################################################

quality:

  maintainability

  scalability

  modularity

  consistency

  correctness

  explainability

  reproducibility

  interoperability

###########################################################
# SUCCESS CRITERIA
###########################################################

success:

  Every compiler module follows these principles.

  Every generated dataset satisfies these principles.

  Every future extension remains compatible.

  Every engineering artifact preserves these principles.

###########################################################
# FUTURE COMPATIBILITY
###########################################################

future:

  immutable_principles: true

  extensible_architecture: true

  backward_compatible: true

  deterministic_execution: true

  graph_native: true

  ontology_driven: true
```
